{
  // Read only by the test runner (scripts/run-tests.mjs, via TSX_TSCONFIG_PATH)
  // -- never by typecheck or the build, both of which resolve tsconfig.json as
  // normal. The runner has no bundler to hand the JSX transform to, so it
  // needs the automatic runtime to actually execute a component; the app's
  // real "jsx": "preserve" is correct for the bundler and stays untouched.
  //
  // The ui and agent-chat packages are listed because their exports maps
  // publish raw `.tsx` source rather than a built boundary, so a shared
  // component rendered under this runner executes those files inside this
  // program.
  //
  // agent-chat joined the list on 10.09.2026, the first time an app test
  // rendered one of its components rather than only importing its types. The
  // symptom was the one described below, from the component's own frame:
  // "React is not defined" out of a file that imports nothing but `forwardRef`.
  // Nothing in that file is wrong, and nothing in the app's own configuration
  // is either -- which is why it is worth naming here rather than diagnosing
  // again.
  //
  // What listing them changes: the JSX transform those files get. tsx loads
  // exactly one tsconfig per process -- the one named here -- and asks it about
  // each file it transforms. A file this `include` does not claim is handed to
  // esbuild with NO tsconfig at all, not with its own package's: esbuild's
  // defaults then apply, and its default for JSX is the classic
  // React.createElement transform. (tsx builds a files-matcher from the single
  // config it loaded and passes its result straight through as `tsconfigRaw`;
  // for an unclaimed file that result is undefined.)
  //
  // So dropping this entry breaks most of what the entry claims. Measured by
  // handing every file to esbuild's classic setting (26.08.2026): 121 of the
  // 123 `.tsx` files under packages/ui/src emit React.createElement that way,
  // and 69 of those 121 have no runtime binding named React to satisfy it --
  // they fail with "React is not defined". Of the 58 flat components in
  // components/ui, 11 are in that set (aspect-ratio, button-group, collapsible,
  // empty, field, kbd, logo-loader, resizable, skeleton, sonner, spinner), and
  // 10 of those 11 are re-exported through ext.ts, so an extension can reach
  // them. skeleton and spinner name React.ComponentProps as a type only, which
  // typechecks against the ambient namespace and leaves nothing in scope at run
  // time. That is the case the automatic runtime exists for, and the one this
  // entry protects.
  //
  // It has never been about how these files' imports resolve; the package
  // states that itself, by importing by package name.
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "jsx": "react-jsx"
  },
  "include": [
    "**/*.ts",
    "**/*.tsx",
    "../../packages/ui/src/**/*.ts",
    "../../packages/ui/src/**/*.tsx",
    "../../packages/agent-chat/src/**/*.ts",
    "../../packages/agent-chat/src/**/*.tsx"
  ]
}
